, "_data": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}], "id": 1}], "id": 0}], "type": "RSLT", "template_override": 1, "id": 10}, {"stimuli_ids": [], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Now, it's time for you to explain the implementation of the 4+1 view model in a company. "}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Take Away"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}, {"type": "text", "content": ""}], "correct_feedback": "", "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}, {"type": "text", "content": ""}], "allowed_attempts": 2}}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment."}], "type": "post_instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "skill", "content": "Reading"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Writing"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Speaking"}], "type": "learning_skills"}], "questions": [{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "pdf", "content": "enpe8n5le01ob11re7pdf01.pdf"}], "type": "question"}], "responses": [{"correct": 1, "selected": 0, "_data": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}], "id": 0}], "id": 0}], "type": "DWS", "template_override": 1, "id": 11}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "type": "welcome", "contents": [{}], "id": 0, "template_override": 1}, {"_data": [], "title": "", "type": "audio", "template_override": 1, "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe8n5le01ob11re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "100", "image": "enpe8n5le01ob11re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Joshua: </span> Hi Emily, what's that diagram you are doing there for? </br><span class=\"strong\">Emily: </span> This? It's just a piping diagram to explain how, where and what the program, I've just designed for the company, is doing.</br><span class=\"strong\">Joshua: </span> Interesting, but I still don't understand plenty of things you've done there, like for example that diagram. </br><span class=\"strong\">Emily: </span> Well, this is just the process view of a whole architecture view model, called 4+1, which allows me to break all the software architecture in multiple views. </br>"}, {"time": "27000", "image": "enpe8n5le01ob11re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Joshua: </span> Wow, I think you've said enough, that's a lot of information to work with for me, I'm kind of baffled by what you've said. </br><span class=\"strong\">Emily: </span> Hehe, don't worry, I will break it down for you. A 4+1 model allows me to diagram what my program does, how it does it and where it is supposed to work. If you see, the model is divided into 4 different views, the first one is the logical view, as an overall, it describes the object model of the design. The second view is the process view, which describes step by step the relation each activity has within the system, which are a lot by the way."}, {"time": "61000", "image": "enpe8n5le01ob11re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "On the other hand, we have the physical view, as you can relate, it describes the mapping of software onto hardware and the distribution aspects, you know, it's like having a computer or a server working only one function of all software and the relation between that station with the others. Finally, we have the development view that shows what the system looks like from the programmer's perspective, that is, software management. </br><span class=\"strong\">Joshua: </span> And what is the +1? </br><span class=\"strong\">Emily: </span> Oh right, that's the relation they all have in scenarios where you can test how they are working."}], "coverimg": "enpe8n5le01ob11re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "id": 0, "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}], "type": "lab_options"}], "type": "scholar"}]}